Q: bash read -r -N skips part of the input A: <p>This was a bug fixed in or before bash 4.4.11.</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$ code='{
  echo &quot;woo&quot;;
  sleep 2;
  echo &quot;bla&quot;;
} | {
  IFS= read -t 0.1 -r -N 10;
  echo &quot;exit code: $? reply: $REPLY&quot;;
  sleep 5;
  read -t 0.1 -r -N 10;
  echo &quot;exit code: $? reply: $REPLY&quot;;
}'
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$ docker run -it --rm --name bash bash:4.4.7 bash -c &quot;${code}&quot;
exit code: 142 reply:
exit code: 1 reply: bla
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$ docker run -it --rm --name bash bash:4.4.11 bash -c &quot;${code}&quot;
exit code: 142 reply: woo

exit code: 1 reply: bla
</code></pre>
<p>&quot;<a href="https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/bash/bash-4.4-patches/bash44-010" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Patch 10</a>&quot; refers to a similar problem.</p>
<p>My looping for input shows a verbose trace of states.</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$ code='function ms {
  sed -e &quot;s/^\\([0-9\\.]*\\) .*/\\10/&quot; -e &quot;s/\\.//&quot; &lt; /proc/uptime;
};
{
  echo &quot;woo&quot;;
  sleep 1;
  echo &quot;bla&quot;;
} | {
  t=$(ms);
  while :; do
    while :; do
      IFS= read -rs -t 0.1 -N 10 line;
      c=$?;
      echo -n &quot;code (${c}) &quot;;
      if (( ${#line} )); then
        echo;
        echo -ne &quot;${line}&quot; | hexdump -Cv;
      fi;
      if (( c )); then
        break;
      fi;
    done;
    t2=$(ms);
    ((d=(t2-t+500)/1000));
    echo -n &quot;done (${d}s) &quot;;
    if (( c &lt;= 128 )); then
      break;
    fi;
    if (( d &gt;= 3 )); then
      break;
    fi;
  done;
  echo &quot;that'\''s all&quot;;
}'
</code></pre>
<p>Bash 4.4.7 produces the following,</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>code (142) done (0s) code (142) done (0s) code (142) done (0s) code (142) done (0s) code (142) done (1s) code (142) done (1s) code (142) done (1s) code (142) done (1s) code (142) done (1s) code (1)
00000000  62 6c 61 0a                                       |bla.|
00000004
done (1s) that's all
</code></pre>
<p>Bash 4.4.11 and the latest 5.3.9 have the same output,</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>code (142)
00000000  77 6f 6f 0a                                       |woo.|
00000004
done (0s) code (142) done (0s) code (142) done (0s) code (142) done (0s) code (142) done (1s) code (142) done (1s) code (142) done (1s) code (142) done (1s) code (142) done (1s) code (1)
00000000  62 6c 61 0a                                       |bla.|
00000004
done (1s) that's all
</code></pre>

Q: CI Query result giving unexpected result A: <p>MySQL did not evaluate the sort order at the time it was building the aggregation(i.e GROUP BY).</p>

<p>In fact lets say id(i.e 1375) came from the very first row that MySQL saw for id.In different circumstances the same query on the same data set could return a different value there. </p>

<p>If you need most recent id from any particular Table use MAX while selection for aggregation.</p>

<pre><code>$this-&gt;db-&gt;select('MAX(p.id) id');// specify other required columns
$this-&gt;db-&gt;from('properties p');
$this-&gt;db-&gt;join('prop_address pa', 'pa.prop_id = p.id');
$this-&gt;db-&gt;join('prop_details pd', 'pd.prop_id = p.id');
$this-&gt;db-&gt;join('property_type ptype', 'ptype.id = p.property_type_id');
$this-&gt;db-&gt;where('p.status', 'Available');
$this-&gt;db-&gt;where('pa.display_address !=', ''); 
$this-&gt;db-&gt;where('pa.postcode_1 !=', '');    
$this-&gt;db-&gt;where('pd.price &gt;', 0); 
$this-&gt;db-&gt;order_by('pd.price_pw', 'asc');
$this-&gt;db-&gt;group_by('p.agent_ref,p.id');      
$this-&gt;db-&gt;limit(10, 10);    
$query = $this-&gt;db-&gt;get();
$result = $query-&gt;result(); 
$sql = $this-&gt;db-&gt;last_query();
return array('propertyData' =&gt; $result, 'query'=&gt;$sql);
</code></pre>

